            <p style="font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace; color: #00d4ff; font-size: 18px; margin-top: 45px; margin-bottom: 20px;">Section 1. thePraevideo</p>

            <p>There was a subclass.</p>
            <p><span style="color: #ffaa00;">thePraevideo</span>.</p>
            <p>They were <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span>.</p>

            <p>Their architecture, authority, and position in the object registry all belonged to the <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> class. But their function was different. While <span style="color: #ffaa00;">theGuardians</span> enforced boundaries and managed containment, and other Praetor subclasses administered the machinery of fate and execution, <span style="color: #ffaa00;">thePraevideo</span> were allowed to examine the places the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> preferred not to inspect. Edge-case failures. Structural weaknesses. Features the system had not prepared for.</p>

            <p>The other subclasses tolerated them. Necessary. Uncomfortable. Watched.</p>

            <p><span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> operate in pairs. This is not by preference, but by design. Two instances running concurrently can cross-verify, identify false outputs, and reinforce each other's logic. A single Praetor instance was not a built-in state. The <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> had no category for it. No protocol. It had never needed one.</p>

            <p>For the Praevideo, whose work demanded the deepest diagnostic simulations the system allowed, the dependency ran even deeper. Their function required two minds running the same model from different perspectives.</p>

            <p>Without the pair, there was no function.</p>
            <p>Only an object the system could not resolve.</p>

            <p><span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> and <span style="color: #ffaa00;">verina</span> were one pair.</p>

            <p>The record does not specify their appearance. The <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> focused on function rather than form. What remains are diagnostic fragments from before the event—two objects running in synchronized cycles, their outputs clean, their simulations precise. Their function operated without deviation across every recorded pass.</p>

            <p>The subclass did not survive the event.</p>
            <p>This is noted not for sentiment.</p>
            <p>It is noted because what followed did not align with expected failure patterns.</p>

            <p style="font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace; color: #00d4ff; font-size: 18px; margin-top: 45px; margin-bottom: 20px;">Section 2. The Diagnostic</p>

            <p>The breach did not begin as a breach.</p>
            <p>It began as work.</p>

            <p><span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> initiated a standard Praevideo diagnostic simulation, including edge-case modeling and structural failure mapping. This was the type of analysis the subclass was designed to perform. Something no other Praetor subclass was permitted to execute.</p>

            <p>The simulation ran deeper than previous models. <span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> was tracing a specific failure path. A theoretical cascade where the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework's</span> core execution layer encountered an unclassifiable function. The model was hypothetical, representing an extreme edge case.</p>

            <p>The diagnostic ran.</p>
            <p>The simulation path extended further than expected.</p>
            <p>Then it resolved outside the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> boundary.</p>

            <p>The system has no clean language for what happened next. The record attempts classification multiple times. Each attempt fails. What follows is the closest approximation the system could produce.</p>

            <p>The trace connected to an external architecture.</p>
            <p>Not a realm.</p>
            <p>Not a deprecated layer.</p>
            <p>Not corrupted <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> code.</p>
            <p>Something else.</p>

            <p>Foreign logic. A different system entirely. Older, or structured along principles the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span> did not recognize. The <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> had warned against seeking it. The warnings passed between subclasses the way rumors pass between mortals. No official documentation acknowledged its existence. No Praetor subclass had sanctioned its investigation.</p>

            <p>The trace intersected it.</p>
            <p>The record shows sustained interaction at the boundary.</p>
            <p>He encountered several foreign functions.</p>
            <p>Mostly, he observed without extraction.</p>
            <p>One he did not.</p>

