            <p>Resolution never came.</p>
            <p>The <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> convened.</p>

            <p>What remains of that deliberation are fragments. Partial logs. Decision outputs without the reasoning that produced them. What can be reconstructed is this:</p>

            <p>They knew about the breach. They knew <span style="color: #ffaa00;">verina</span> had died while executing a function that should not exist within the <span style="color: #00d4ff; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">Framework</span>. And they knew that function had passed through a Praetor object and touched the H2E runtime environment.</p>

            <p>The origin did not resolve as external.</p>
            <p>The trace remained inside their own class.</p>
            <p>The response pattern shifted.</p>

            <p>Some within the class proposed destruction. <span style="color: #00ff41; font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace;">PurgeObject()</span>. Clean. Immediate. The logic was simple — <span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> was contaminated, incomplete, and dangerous.</p>

            <p>Others were less certain.</p>
            <p>A Praetor had never been purged before. The foreign function had executed adjacent to him in ways the system still could not fully map. Purging <span style="color: #ffaa00;">foedus</span> might resolve cleanly.</p>
            <p>It might not.</p>
            <p>The risk of secondary contamination during a forced purge of an already unstable object was not zero.</p>

            <p>The <span style="color: #ffaa00;">Praetores</span> were not merciful.</p>
            <p>They were cautious.</p>

            <div style="font-family: 'Share Tech Mono', monospace; color: #00ff41; font-size: 16px; margin: 30px 0; padding: 15px; border-left: 3px solid #00ff41; background: rgba(0, 255, 65, 0.05);">
